174	 * the address in the admin visitor log is whatever the visitor chose to claim.
175	 *
176	 * The trustworthy entry is the one NGINX appended: the last one. Everything to
177	 * its left is client-authored and is discarded. `x-real-ip` (set by the same
178	 * vhosts with `proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr`, which REPLACES rather
179	 * than appends) is an equally sound second source and is used when no
180	 * X-Forwarded-For arrives at all.
181	 *
182	 * Accepts anything with a `.get()` — `await headers()` from a server action,
183	 * `req.headers`, or the `Request` itself — so there is ONE definition of "who is
184	 * calling" rather than a copy per call site that can drift.
185	 *
186	 * Every candidate is VALIDATED as an IP before it is returned. A header that is
187	 * not an address is not an identity, and letting one through means an attacker
188	 * picks their own bucket name by sending garbage — the same defeat as reading
189	 * the first hop, one step removed. When nothing checks out the caller falls into
190	 * the shared `UNKNOWN_IP` bucket, which is conservative rather than private.
191	 *
192	 * DEPLOYMENT COUPLING: correct for exactly `TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS` reverse proxies
